Which of the following BEST describes what the above resource policy does?', '<p>A company is using the AWS Directory Service to integrate its on-premises Microsoft Active Directory (AD) domain with its Amazon EC2 instances via an AD connector. The IAM identities, which are members of an AD group, have the following identity‑based policy attached to allow Directory Service access:</p><pre class="prettyprint linenums"> 
{ 
   "Version":"2012-10-17", 
   "Statement":[ 
      {
         "Sid":"DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234", 
         "Effect":"Allow", 
         "Action":[
            "ds:*"
         ], 
         "Resource":"arn:aws:ds:us-east-1:987654321012:directory/d-1234567890"
      }, 
    {
} 
</pre><p>Which of the following BEST describes what the above resource policy does?</p>', '[{"key":"A","text":"Allows all Directory Service ds calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: d-1234567890"},{"key":"B","text":"Allows all Directory Service ds calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234"},{"key":"C","text":"Allows all Directory Service ds calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: 987654321012"},{"key":"D","text":"Allows all Directory Service ds calls as long as the resource contains the directory name of: DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234"}]', ARRAY['A']::text[], '<p><strong>AWS Directory Service</strong> provides multiple ways to use Amazon Cloud Directory and Microsoft Active Directory (AD) with other AWS services. Directories store information about users, groups, and devices, and administrators use them to manage access to information and resources. AWS Directory Service provides multiple directory choices for customers who want to use existing Microsoft AD or Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)–aware applications in the cloud. It also offers those same choices to developers who need a directory to manage users, groups, devices, and access.</p><p><img alt="AWS Directory Service" height="480" src="https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/AWS Directory Service -21APR2026.png" width="1000"></p><p>Every AWS resource is owned by an AWS account, and permissions to create or access the resources are governed by permissions policies. An account administrator can attach permissions policies to IAM identities (that is, users, groups, and roles), and some services (such as AWS Lambda) also support attaching permissions policies to resources.</p><p>The following resource policy example allows all <code>ds</code> calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID "<code>d-1234567890</code>".</p><pre class="prettyprint linenums">{
 "Version":"2012-10-17",
 "Statement":[
    {
        "Sid":"VisualEditor0",
        "Effect":"Allow",
        "Action":[
            "ds:*"
         ],
                 "Resource":"arn:aws:ds:us-east-1:123456789012:directory/d-1234567890"
    },
    {
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Action":[
        "ec2:*"
    ],
    "Resource":"*"
    }
  ]
}</pre><p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong>Allows all Directory Service </strong><code><strong>(ds)</strong></code><strong> calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: </strong><code><strong>d-1234567890</strong></code>.</p><p>The option that says: <strong>Allows all Directory Service </strong><code><strong>(ds)</strong></code><strong> calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: </strong><code><strong>DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234</strong></code> is incorrect because <code>DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234</code> is the Statement ID (SID) and not the Directory ID.</p><p>The option that says: <strong>Allows all Directory Service </strong><code><strong>(ds)</strong></code><strong> calls as long as the resource contains the directory ID: </strong><code><strong>987654321012</strong></code> is incorrect because the numbers: <code>987654321012</code> is primarily an Account ID and not the Directory ID.</p><p>The option that says: <strong>Allows all Directory Service </strong><code><strong>(ds)</strong></code><strong> calls as long as the resource contains the directory name of: </strong><code><strong>DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234</strong></code> is incorrect because <code>DirectoryTutorialsDojo1234</code> is simply the Statement ID (SID) and not the Directory name.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>References:</strong></p><p><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directoryservice/latest/admin-guide/IAM_Auth_Access_IdentityBased.html">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directoryservice/latest/admin-guide/IAM_Auth_Access_IdentityBased.html</a></p><p><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directoryservice/latest/admin-guide/IAM_Auth_Access_Overview.html">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directoryservice/latest/admin-guide/IAM_Auth_Access_Overview.html</a></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Check out this AWS Identity &amp; Access Management (IAM) Cheat Sheet:</strong></p><p><a href="https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/?src=udemy">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam</a></p>', 'Design Secure Architectures', ARRAY['https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/AWS Directory Service -21APR2026.png']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;


